<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> jack asked me some more about 8051 pF, shall I do a little 
advertising in public (am I allowed :-)
<[Gary] GARY-S> sysgod asks 'am i allowed?'
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> only when I am advertising Forth, Inc. <grin>
<[Gary] GARY-S> It's my conference - hit it !
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ok, Jack asked if 8051 was running polyFORTH ISD-4
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ISD-4 stands for Integrated Development System
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> (polly4th for dyslexics?)
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> but the 8051 runs under chipFORTH, which is a subset of 
ISD-4
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS>  :-)
<[Gary] GARY-S> postfix acronyms
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> chipFORTH is an umbilical support system
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> where you target compile to the 8051 from a PC
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> and then interactively test over the umbilical
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> it is pretty neat, but not a full up pF system
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> i.e. you have no disk or terminal directly attached
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> e.g. you can not say BLOCK on the target unless you
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> provide the hardware support yourself
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> So how diff the targ compiler from a metacompiler?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> Target Compiling is the name that Forth, Inc. uses for what 
F83 etc calls meta compiling
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> the difference is that you can create PROMs or whatever you 
want
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> So what you are doing is meta/target compiling 
anything from an app to a tiny Forth system onto the 8051?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> you can even compile a CPU different than the host
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> exactly
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> And all compiling is actually taking place on PC 
(until you have tiny sys up & running?)
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ALL compiling happens on the host
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> no compiler that can run on the target side is provided in 
chipFORTH systems
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> And you don't have to compile a sys, you can compile a 
little application if you are hip to the art?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> it can be as small as you want it to be
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> it doesn't even have to be Forth
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> This is a world o' diff from the idea of a target 
compiler as the sense of the word is being taken in X3J14.
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> how so?
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> Well, in general the tendency is becoming to view a 
target compiler ...
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> as a compiler which generates binary for a target from 
an ANS Forth program that could equally well have been ...
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> loaded interpretively on an interpretive ANS Forth 
system.
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> You dig the difference?
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> ga
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I can see how their definition could be an extension of pF's
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> the biggest difference is that they imply you are also 
compiling the nucleus
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> with pF's Target compiler's that does not have to be the 
case
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> I understood it the opposite way! But now I guess you 
are saying ...
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> Nope, don't understand. To use chipForth don't you 
have to compile a kernel of sorts?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> The 8051 nucleus is included in the package
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> so you can compile a Forth application
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> compiled or in source?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> in source
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> but you don't have to use it
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> you could just as easily write the entire
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> application in assembler
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> and use the chipFORTH to compile it
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> and test it
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> ga
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> in which case you gots yourself a $$$$.%% cross 
assembler :-)
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> exactly :-)
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I should mention that the chipFORTH umbilical
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> is written in Forth, so if you want to
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> test using the umbilical, ya kinda have
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> to include the nucleus and the talker code
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> is it the same chipForth that does the 68HC11?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> yes, only the nucleus is really any different
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> the source nucleus for the target?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> we also do the 8096/196
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> you don't get the nucleus for the host in cF
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> only the source for the target
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> Aha, it's just pF sans source.
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> and sans options
<[Gary] GARY-S> You still there Deano ?
<[Deano] D.MARTINDAL1> still here
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> how big is the chipForth executable?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> gee, I don't know but likely less than 6K
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> so it is really a minimal pF.
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> were you asking about the host executable or the targets?
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> host. but tell about both, if you have time.
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> the target is the one under 6K
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> the host is the standard pF with some of the
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> option source code removed from the source file
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> i.e graphics, math, etc
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS>  6k for the target is akshooally kinda largish.
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I believe the pF is around 30 K or so
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> well, I am only gessing there
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> the nucleus is a full up Forth kernal
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> but you can also trim it down to as small as you need
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> which you can mix and match, as it were, discard 2@, 
for instance?
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> yes, you can trim anything that you are not using
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> But not my beard!
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS>  :-)
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> I said not using, I use my beard all the time (to hide 
behind :-)
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS>  You ever meet Chuck Curley? He is aptly named! :-)
<[Dennis] D.RUFFER> not yet
<[Gary] GARY-S> jax is gonna start having to come his back, up and over :-)
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> He's out here now with us.
<[jax@well] FIGCHAPTERS> anyway, tnx Dennis for chipForth toot oriole.
